  "body": "## What was wrong\n\nThe routing cards clipped the thing they exist to show. Measured on a 1500px window: four cards fitted the row at 365px each, leaving **169px** for the model ref and truncating **16 of 18** of them mid-name — `nous_portal/meit`, `chatgpt_oauth/gp`. The reorder arrows shipped in 5.37.0 made it slightly worse, since the primary field now carries a button cluster too.\n\n## What changed\n\n**1. Fewer, wider cards.** `.route-grid` asks for 460px rather than 360px, so three rails fit that window instead of four and each card gets 491px.\n\n**2. The chrome around the text is trimmed to what it needs** — this is where the rest came from, and it's the half that doesn't cost density:\n\n| | before | after |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| row buttons | 30px wide | 26px (height untouched, target stays 30px tall) |\n| marker column | 26px | 24px |\n| row gaps | 6px | 4px |\n| card side padding | 20px | 14px |\n| chevron gutter | 44px | 28px |\n| field inset | 14px | 10px |\n\nAt the same window: **337px of field, 0 of 18 clipped**, including `nvidia_nim/nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b` — the longest a default install can produce.\n\n**The one non-obvious part:** the button rules had to be written against the `.route-rail`-scoped overrides, not the generic `.model-chain-*` ones. The generic rules are shadowed on this page, so editing them changes nothing here. A first pass did exactly that and measured as a **no-op** — the numbers only moved once the probe used `!important`, which is what gave it away.\n\n**3. A `title` on every model field**, equal to its value. A ref longer than the field is now recoverable by hovering rather than only by clicking in and scrolling. That is what covers a user whose provider names run longer than mine — the alternative was tuning the pixel budget to my own longest model name, which is fitting to the sample. Reorders assign `.value` directly and fire no event, so they now dispatch `change`; otherwise the title and the dirty state would go stale after a swap.\n\n## Verification\n\nMeasured in a **real browser** (Chrome DevTools) against a real server at the reported 1506px grid width, on a config seeded to match the report:\n\n```\nbefore   4 cols   365px card   169px field   16/18 clipped (worst 179px short)\nA only   3 cols   491px card   295px field    3/18 clipped\nfinal    3 cols   491px card   337px field    0/18 clipped\n```\n\nRail alignment re-checked inside one card after resizing the marker column — marker, all three row numbers and the add \"+\" share a centre at 27px. Tooltips present on 18/18 fields.\n\nThe 5.37.0 reorder harness re-run against this build: **29/29 passing**. It first reported 6 failures against the seeded server — that was the harness assuming an empty config, not a regression; re-run against an empty home it is green.\n\nLocal CI: `ruff format --check`, `ruff check`, `ty` clean; pytest **4795 passed** with the documented 6 `test_install_ps1_*` baseline failures.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **Three columns instead of four is a density trade** on a wide window. It was the explicit choice: at four columns nothing fits, and cards stretch to fill the row so there is no way to have four columns *and* readable refs.\n- **Verified at one width.** 1506px of grid, which is the reported one. Narrower windows fall to two columns then one, which is the same behaviour as before with a higher breakpoint.\n- **No new guard test.** The existing class guard already fails if these rules are lost in a merge, and a test asserting pixel values would encode my window rather than an invariant.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
